```json
{
  "shoebox_sort": {
    "method": "One pass, eight piles. Write the category on any receipt where it isn't obvious, then total each pile.",
    "categories": [
      {"pile": "Fuel", "goes_here": "Gas station receipts for trucks and equipment"},
      {"pile": "Materials", "goes_here": "Plants, mulch, stone, irrigation parts - job materials"},
      {"pile": "Repairs and maintenance", "goes_here": "Mower blades, truck service, small-engine repair"},
      {"pile": "Supplies and consumables", "goes_here": "Gloves, bags, oil, string, blades"},
      {"pile": "Equipment purchases", "goes_here": "Anything that lasts years, not weeks - keep these receipts separately forever"},
      {"pile": "Fees and services", "goes_here": "Dump fees, permits, software, phone"},
      {"pile": "Meals and crew", "goes_here": "Crew lunches and water/ice - keep, and let the accountant rule on treatment"},
      {"pile": "Personal - out", "goes_here": "Anything that isn't the business. It will be in there. Pull it now, not in March."}
    ],
    "tie_out": "Add the seven business piles. The sum should match the business total on the card statement for the same period, minus any cash purchases. If it's off, the difference is a missing receipt or a personal charge still in a business pile - find it while the year is fresh."
  }
}
```

Photograph each pile before you hand anything over. Thermal receipts fade within a year or two, and the photo is your backup when the paper goes blank.

```json
{
  "missing_receipt_log": {
    "columns": ["date", "merchant", "amount", "likely_purpose", "how_verified", "status"],
    "example_rows": [
      {"date": "2026-03-14", "merchant": "Salon supply warehouse", "amount": 143.62, "likely_purpose": "Color restock", "how_verified": "Inventory shelf count and color order history", "status": "documented, no paper"},
      {"date": "2026-05-02", "merchant": "Online beauty supply", "amount": 58.20, "likely_purpose": "Retail shelf restock", "how_verified": "Order confirmation found in email", "status": "receipt recovered"},
      {"date": "2026-09-21", "merchant": "Hardware store", "amount": 27.45, "likely_purpose": "Sink repair parts", "how_verified": "Calendar shows plumber visit same day", "status": "documented, no paper"}
    ],
    "rules": [
      "One row per unmatched statement charge - never lump",
      "Purpose comes from evidence (inventory, calendar, photos), never a guess dressed as fact; write 'unconfirmed' when it is",
      "Total the log monthly so the gap never grows past one month of charges"
    ]
  }
}
```

The 'how verified' column is the whole point. A charge with a plausible evidence trail is a documented expense with missing paper; a charge with nothing is a question. Keeping those two visibly different is what makes the log useful to your accountant instead of just tidy.

```json
{
  "receipt_matching_summary_2026": {
    "total_card_charges": 22614.00,
    "receipts_matched": 20908.00,
    "unmatched_total": 1706.00,
    "unmatched_breakdown": [
      {"category": "Receipts recovered from email or merchant accounts after the first pass", "amount": 912.00, "note": "Digital copies attached"},
      {"category": "Documented in missing-receipt log with purpose and evidence", "amount": 563.00, "note": "Log attached, 14 charges"},
      {"category": "Personal charges on the business card, excluded", "amount": 231.00, "note": "Listed separately, not claimed as business"}
    ],
    "check": "20,908 + 912 + 563 + 231 = 22,614 - every statement dollar is accounted for in one of four states"
  }
}
```

Send the summary with the missing-receipt log and the personal-charges list attached. The number your accountant cares about isn't the matched percentage — it's that matched + recovered + documented + excluded adds back to the full statement total with nothing unexplained. What to *do* about the documented-but-paperless $563 is their call; your summary gives them the clean fact pattern to rule on.

The problem isn't receipts — it's that one statement serves two people's memory. Split the trail:

```json
{
  "two_cardholder_system": [
    {"fix": "Separate card numbers on the same account", "why": "Most issuers give each cardholder a distinct number, so the statement shows whose card made each charge. This one change ends most of the archaeology."},
    {"fix": "One shared receipt inbox", "why": "A single email address or shared album both of you send receipt photos to the day of purchase, with a one-word note (paint, client lunch, van repair). One searchable inbox beats interviewing each other in January."},
    {"fix": "Weekly five-minute sweep, own charges only", "why": "Each person flags their own charges lacking a receipt photo. A week-old purchase is findable; a ten-month-old one is folklore."},
    {"fix": "Monthly tie-out, alternating who runs it", "why": "Charges = receipts + documented missing + personal. Alternating keeps both partners fluent in the system."},
    {"fix": "Bright line for personal charges", "why": "They happen; the rule is they are declared in the sweep, listed, and excluded - never quietly absorbed into a business category."}
  ],
  "for_this_year": "Reconstruct month by month with the same equation, each of you taking your own charges. One evening of work, and the last time if the new system starts in January."
}
```

```json
{
  "bakery_expense_summary_2026": {
    "categories": [
      {"category": "Ingredients", "receipts_total": 6214.00},
      {"category": "Packaging", "receipts_total": 1108.00},
