    "flagged": "Three W-9s outstanding. Chase order by amount: cleaner, snow removal, sign painter."
  }
}
```

Your five payees total $9,940. Send this summary as-is, with the request history for the three missing forms — who needs a 1099 and what happens with the missing W-9s is your accountant's ruling, and this table gives them the amounts and the paper status in one glance. Keep chasing in the order flagged; the cleaner's $1,800 is the biggest undocumented chunk.

Keep two lists, built from different sources:

```json
{
  "employees": {
    "source": "The payroll system, not bank payments",
    "record": "Per person: gross wages, withholdings, employer taxes for the year",
    "year_end_items": ["Payroll annual summary", "All four quarterly filings", "W-2s come out of the same system"]
  },
  "contractors": {
    "source": "Payment records you assemble yourself - no payroll record exists",
    "record": "Per person: every payment totaled, payment methods, W-9 status",
    "year_end_items": ["Payee list with totals", "W-9 chase log", "This list is what feeds 1099 readiness"]
  },
  "mixing_traps": [
    "A person on both lists in one year (contractor hired mid-year): keep them on both with date ranges noted, do not merge, flag it for the accountant",
    "Paying a contractor through payroll 'to make it easy', or an employee off the books 'as a contractor': the lists should reflect how the work was actually arranged, not the payment plumbing",
    "Bonuses or gifts to contractors folded into a payments total without a note"
  ],
  "boundary": "Which list a person belongs on is a classification ruling for your accountant, made from how the work is directed and structured. Your deliverable is two clean lists with honest facts."
}
```

```json
{
  "payee_record": {
    "payee": "Marketing consultant",
    "period": "2026",
    "payments": [
      {"pattern": "January through October", "count": 10, "amount_each": 750, "subtotal": 7500},
      {"pattern": "November and December", "count": 2, "amount_each": 900, "subtotal": 1800}
    ],
    "total_paid_2026": 9300,
    "w9_on_file": "verify before filing season",
    "notes": "Rate increased in November - keep the email or contract amendment that documents the change with this record, so the two rates don't look like an error."
  }
}
```

Total for the year: $7,500 + $1,800 = **$9,300**. Two habits in this record worth copying: the rate change is explained in the record itself (an unexplained mid-year jump invites questions), and the W-9 status says 'verify' rather than assuming — check it now, while there's time to chase it if it's missing.

Yes — record them, and mark the entity type. The list is a complete record of who you paid for services; completeness is your job, and the filtering is your accountant's.

```json
{
  "payee_list_rules": {
    "one_row_per_payee": ["Total paid", "Payment methods", "W-9 status", "Entity type as shown on the W-9: individual, LLC, S-corp, C-corp, partnership, unknown"],
    "w9_from_businesses_too": "The W-9 is what establishes entity type. Without one, 'they are incorporated' is your impression, not a record.",
    "not_only_services": "Rent to a landlord, payments to an attorney, and merchandise purchases ride on the same list with a nature-of-payment note - several have their own reporting treatment for your accountant to sort out.",
    "do_not_pre_filter": "An accountant would rather rule on twelve rows than discover a thirteenth payee in your bank statement in February."
  }
}
```

The honest list, entity types attached, is the deliverable.

```json
{
  "mileage_summary_2026": {
    "trip_patterns": [
      {"purpose": "Supply runs", "round_trip_miles": 14, "trips": 22, "subtotal_miles": 308},
      {"purpose": "Client site visits", "round_trip_miles": 31, "trips": 9, "subtotal_miles": 279},
      {"purpose": "Bank and post office", "round_trip_miles": 6, "trips": 15, "subtotal_miles": 90}
    ],
    "total_business_miles": 677,
    "method_note": "Pattern-based summary. For the underlying log, attach the dated trip list - a pattern summary supports the total but the dated entries are the record. Any trip that was combined with a personal stop should be listed as driven, with the personal detour noted."
  }
}
```

The math: 22 × 14 = 308, 9 × 31 = 279, 15 × 6 = 90 — **677 business miles** for the year. Keep the dated list behind this summary; if a trip's distance varied (a second stop, a detour), the dated entry should show the actual miles rather than the pattern number, with the summary adjusted.

```json
{
  "mileage_reconstruction_Q1": {
    "documented_trips": [
      {"purpose": "Supply trips", "trips": 18, "round_trip_miles": 12, "subtotal": 216},
      {"purpose": "Client trips", "trips": 7, "round_trip_miles": 26, "subtotal": 182}
    ],
    "documented_total_miles": 398,
    "gaps": [
      {"period": "Two weeks in February", "status": "No calendar entries and no receipts found", "treatment": "Left blank in the log. Not estimated. If the business was closed or you were away, note that; if trips happened, they are undocumented and stay out."}
    ],
    "method": "Reconstructed from calendar entries with map-tool distances, [date]. Corroboration: fuel receipts dated within the documented weeks.",
    "label": "RECONSTRUCTED LOG - not contemporaneous"
  }
}
```

Documented Q1 total: 216 + 182 = **398 miles**, with a named hole in February. That hole is a feature, not a failure: a reconstructed log with an honest gap reads as credible, while one that smoothly covers all thirteen weeks reads as invented. Give this to your accountant with the method note attached and let them rule on its use.

```json
{
  "vehicle_summary_2026": {
    "vehicle": "Work truck (business and personal use)",
    "mileage": {
      "business_miles_logged": 11208,
      "total_miles": 12030,
      "business_share": "93.2%",
      "basis": "Trip log for business miles; Jan 1 and Dec 31 odometer photos for total"
    },
    "cost_records": [
      {"item": "Fuel", "total": 5940},
      {"item": "Repairs and maintenance", "total": 1870},
      {"item": "Insurance", "total": 1640}
    ],
    "cost_records_total": 9450,
    "loan": "Year-end loan statement attached separately - balance and interest figures come from the lender's statement, not from this summary",
    "questions_for_accountant": [
      "With a 93.2% documented business share, which vehicle method do you want to use for this truck?",
      "The truck was bought in August 2025 - is there anything about the first-year records you still need?"
    ]
  }
}
```

The share checks out: 11,208 ÷ 12,030 = 93.2%. Notice what's deliberately *not* in this summary — any dollar figure derived from the miles or the share. Computing the deduction is the accountant's step, using the method they choose; your summary hands them verified inputs and asks the method question out loud.

```json
{
  "income_reconciliation_2026": {
    "booking_system_revenue": 52300,
    "step_1": {
      "adjustment": "Sales recorded in bookings but paid by cash/check outside the processor",
      "amount": -400,
      "result": 51900,
      "label": "Processor gross - matches processor annual statement"
    },
    "step_2": {
      "adjustment": "Processor fees withheld",
      "amount": -1310,
      "result": 50590
    },
    "step_3": {
      "adjustment": "December sales settled in January (timing)",
      "amount": -175,
      "result": 50415,
      "label": "Bank deposits - matches"
    },
    "unexplained_difference": 0,
    "note": "The $400 cash/check layer needs its own deposit trail - list those deposits separately so the -400 is evidenced, not asserted."
  }
}
```

The bridge closes exactly: $52,300 − $400 − $1,310 − $175 = **$50,415**. Each step names its cause and its source document — the processor statement proves steps 1 and 2, the January settlement report proves step 3. That structure, three systems tied with named differences, is a reconciliation your accountant can rely on rather than re-perform.

Work the standard suspects in this order — one of them explains most payout gaps:

```json
{
  "payout_gap_investigation": {
    "processor_payouts": 61200,
    "bank_credits_from_processor": 60050,
    "difference": 1150,
    "suspects_in_order": [
      {"suspect": "Year-boundary payout", "test": "A payout the processor counts as sent Dec 30-31 that the bank posts Jan 2. Check the payout list against bank credits in the first week of January - a $1,150 credit there closes this instantly."},
      {"suspect": "Chargeback or refund debit", "test": "Processors net these against a later payout or pull them as a separate bank debit that does not look like a payout. Search the statement for debits under the processor's name."},
      {"suspect": "Reserve or hold", "test": "Some processors hold a percentage or fixed amount; it shows in their payout figure before it is released, or sits in a balance line."},
      {"suspect": "Payout to a different account", "test": "If you changed bank accounts mid-year, early payouts went to the old one. Total both accounts before concluding anything is missing."},
      {"suspect": "Fees debited separately", "test": "A monthly fee debit rather than netting changes what 'payouts' means on their statement versus your credits."}
    ],
    "deliverable": "A one-line bridge naming the cause: 'Processor payouts $61,200 - payout posted Jan 2 $1,150 = bank $60,050.' If no suspect explains it, the bridge with an 'unexplained $1,150' line goes to the processor's support first (it is their ledger), then to your accountant with the answer attached."
  }
}
```

```json
{
  "refunds_log": {
    "columns": ["date_refunded", "original_sale_date", "client_or_order", "amount", "reason", "channel"],
    "example_rows": [
      {"date_refunded": "2026-02-11", "original_sale_date": "2026-01-28", "client_or_order": "Appointment #4471", "amount": 85.00, "reason": "Service not rendered - stylist illness", "channel": "processor"},
      {"date_refunded": "2026-03-02", "original_sale_date": "2026-02-19", "client_or_order": "Order #881", "amount": 42.50, "reason": "Damaged in shipping", "channel": "processor"},
      {"date_refunded": "2026-03-19", "original_sale_date": "2026-03-19", "client_or_order": "Appointment #4602", "amount": 120.00, "reason": "Chargeback - cardholder dispute", "channel": "processor - under review"}
    ],
    "rules": [
      "Refunds and chargebacks are logged separately - a chargeback can reverse, a refund cannot",
      "Always record the original sale date: a January refund of a December sale crosses the year boundary and your accountant needs to see that",
      "Total the log quarterly and tie it to the processor's refund total; the two should agree to the dollar"
    ]
  }
}
```

With this log, your income bridge gets a real number: gross bookings minus logged refunds and chargebacks, each one traceable to a sale. The cross-year rows are the ones to flag when you hand it over — how they're treated is a timing ruling for your accountant, and the log's two-date format is exactly what lets them make it.

Build a one-page timing schedule — it's the document that makes December-heavy businesses readable:

```json
{
  "timing_schedule": {
    "purpose": "Itemizes the entire difference between sales-basis income (counted when sold) and deposit-basis income (counted when settled)",
    "contents": [
      {"list": "Late-December sales settled in January", "fields": "Sale date, settlement date, gross, fee, net deposited - from the processor's settlement reports", "example": "Dec 29-31 card sales: gross $3,860, fees $104, net $3,756, deposited Jan 2-5"},
      {"list": "Prior December's sales settled this January", "fields": "Same fields, from last year's reports", "note": "This figure comes OUT of a deposit-based view of this year - the bridge needs both ends"}
    ],
    "presentation": "Show both bases side by side: booking/POS total for the year, bank-received total for the year, and this schedule as the itemized difference between them.",
    "evidence": "The processor's settlement reports, kept with the schedule - their dates are what makes the schedule credible rather than a story."
  }
}
```

Which basis your filing uses is your accountant's ruling, and it generally must stay consistent year to year — another reason the schedule matters. What you must not do is quietly pick whichever basis looks nicer; the schedule exists so the choice is deliberate and documented.

Without a booking system, your job calendar becomes the income record's skeleton:

```json
{
  "income_register_build": [
    {"step": "List every job from the calendar", "fields": "Date, client, work done, amount charged - this spreadsheet is your sales register"},
    {"step": "Attach the payment trail per job", "fields": "Method (Venmo, Zelle, check, cash), date received, amount - app histories and bank deposits are searchable by amount and date"},
    {"step": "Flag the mismatches honestly", "examples": "Job with no payment found (unpaid? cash? comped for a relative?) - payment with no job (deposit for future work? tip included?) - each flag gets a one-line resolution or stays flagged"},
    {"step": "Total by method and tie to the bank", "note": "App and check totals should meet the year's deposits; cash gets its own line with a note on how it was tracked"},
    {"step": "Future-work deposits on their own list", "fields": "Client, amount received, scheduled job date - money in hand, work not yet done"}
  ],
  "result": "A register where every dollar of income has a job behind it and every job has a payment status - a stronger record than many booking systems produce. The flags and their resolutions are what let you answer your accountant's completeness questions with a straight face."
}
```

```json
{
  "income_summary_2026": {
    "sources": [
      {"source": "Platform sales (net of refunds, before platform fees)", "amount": 17810},
      {"source": "Direct site sales (net of refunds, before processor fees)", "amount": 9415},
      {"source": "Wholesale orders (invoiced)", "amount": 6200},
      {"source": "Cash sales at markets (from market log)", "amount": 3940}
    ],
    "total_income": 37365,
    "basis_note": "All four figures on the same basis: refunds removed, fees NOT removed (fees appear as their own expense lines), sales counted in the year of sale. Market cash per the dated market log; wholesale per invoice register - attach both.",
    "ties_to": "Bank deposits will be lower than this total by: platform and processor fees, plus December sales settling in January, minus last December's sales that settled this January. The timing/fee bridge is a separate schedule in the packet."
  }
}
```

Total: **$37,365**, with every source on the same stated basis — that basis note is doing real work, because 'net' means different things on different platforms and the note pins it down. Keep the wholesale invoice register and market log attached; they're the evidence for the two sources no processor statement corroborates.

As its own schedule, clearly separated from this year's earned income in everything you hand over:

```json
{
  "customer_deposits_held": {
    "total_collected_for_future_events": 5800,
    "detail": "Per-client list attached: client, event date in 2027, amount paid, balance due, refund terms as stated in the contract",
    "presentation": "Shown as deposits held (money owed as future work), NOT inside 2026 sales totals. The booking system's revenue report must be adjusted by this amount if it counted the deposits as sales when collected.",
    "questions_for_accountant": [
      "How do you want advance deposits treated for this filing, given my contracts and past practice?",
      "Two deposits are partially refundable until 90 days out - does that change anything you need documented?"
    ]
  }
}
```

The per-client list matters as much as the total: it proves the $5,800 is real obligations with names and dates, and it becomes next year's opening schedule. How deposits are treated for tax timing is a genuine ruling — it depends on your method and history — so it goes to your accountant as a question with the schedule attached, never as a quiet decision baked into your sales total.

One row per asset, with the facts a depreciation schedule is built from:

```json
{
  "fixed_asset_list": {
    "columns": ["asset", "date_placed_in_service", "cost", "prior_depreciation_as_given", "status"],
    "example_rows": [
      {"asset": "Styling chair (1 of 2)", "date_placed_in_service": "2021-03-15", "cost": 1450, "prior_depreciation_as_given": 1180, "status": "in use"},
      {"asset": "Washer and dryer set", "date_placed_in_service": "2023-06-01", "cost": 1680, "prior_depreciation_as_given": 720, "status": "in use"},
      {"asset": "POS system and tablet", "date_placed_in_service": "2025-01-20", "cost": 890, "prior_depreciation_as_given": 260, "status": "in use"}
    ],
    "rules": [
      "Cost means what you paid including tax, delivery, and installation - from the receipt, not memory",
      "Date placed in service is when it was ready and used, which can differ from the purchase date - record both if they differ",
      "Prior depreciation comes from last year's return or schedule - copy it as given; never recompute it yourself",
      "Status matters: sold, retired, or broken assets stay on the list with their disposal date until your accountant removes them"
    ]
  }
}
```

Bring last year's depreciation schedule if you have one — the new list should agree with it plus this year's purchases and minus disposals. That agreement check is the fastest way to catch the asset that was sold two years ago and is still riding along.

```json
{
  "asset_summary": {
    "assets": [
      {"asset": "Espresso machine", "year": 2024, "cost": 4800},
      {"asset": "Grinder", "year": 2024, "cost": 950},
      {"asset": "POS system", "year": 2025, "cost": 1150},
      {"asset": "Display case", "year": 2025, "cost": 2300},
      {"asset": "Patio furniture set", "year": 2026, "cost": 780}
    ],
    "total_cost_all_assets": 9980,
    "prior_depreciation_as_given": 2610,
    "note": "Book figure before this year's depreciation: 9,980 - 2,610 = 7,370. This is an arithmetic summary of your records, NOT a valuation and NOT this year's depreciation - the accountant computes the current year from the schedule, methods, and in-service dates."
  }
}
```

Costs total **$9,980**; against the $2,610 prior depreciation as given, the running book figure is $7,370 before this year is computed. Two things to attach: the receipt for the patio set (the only new asset) and last year's schedule showing where the $2,610 came from, item by item — a lump prior-depreciation figure without its schedule forces your accountant to rebuild it.

Leased equipment lives in a different drawer from owned assets — keep it off the fixed-asset list and give it its own schedule:

```json
{
  "leased_equipment_schedule": {
    "per_lease": [
      "The agreement itself: equipment description, start date, term, monthly payment, end-of-lease options (return, buyout price, renewal) - the agreement is the master document",
      "Payments record: twelve monthly payments with dates and amounts, tied to bank statements; any mid-year change (step-up clause, restructured month) noted with its reason",
      "One-line status: e.g. 'Copier lease, month 22 of 36, $189/mo, $1 buyout at term end' vs 'Press lease, month 8 of 60, fair-market buyout' - the end-of-term terms are what your accountant reads first"
    ],
    "kept_separate": [
      "Anything you own outright stays on the asset list - the two schedules side by side give the full equipment picture",
      "Insurance or maintenance the lease obliges you to carry goes in its normal expense category, with a note tying it to the leased item"
    ],
    "boundary": "How a lease is treated - and the buyout wording matters to that ruling - is your accountant's call, made from the agreement. Your job is the agreement plus a clean payment trail, neither buried in the asset list nor mixed into generic equipment expenses."
  }
